Stages: SDD

Purpose:
  Formal registry of all engineering stages used by the SDLC and STLC.
  A stage is a named engineering responsibility with defined inputs,
  outputs, and gate criteria. Stages are the building blocks of chains
  (bax chains/chains.sdd -> Stages, DefaultFlow).

  This file is the authoritative stage list — chains/chains.sdd ->
  Stages is a summary copy for chain context, not the registry itself.


Owns:
  ./

Scope:
  Stage definitions
  Stage purposes
  Stage inputs/outputs
  Stage ownership
  Stage activation conditions


DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific flows (bax project/flows.sdd)
  Project-specific chains (bax chains/chains.sdd)
  Engineering skills (bax skills/)
  Task instances (bax tasks/)
  Project architecture (bax project/architecture.sdd)


Stages:

  Orchestration stages — analysis/decision points that begin or redirect
  a chain:

    AN:     Analysis
      Purpose:  Initial scope, requirement, and constraint analysis.
      Owner:    AR
      Input:    prompt, decision, finding
      Output:   analysis document, scope definition
      Activates: when new work is initiated or existing work is impacted

    AR:     Architecture
      Purpose:  Architecture decisions, boundary drawing, modularity.
      Owner:    AR
      Input:    analysis document, project model
      Output:   architecture decision, module boundaries
      Activates: when new feature requires architecture decisions or
                 existing architecture is impacted

    IMPACT: Impact Analysis
      Purpose:  Determine what a bug or change actually affects before
                touching it.
      Owner:    AR
      Input:    bug report, change request, affected components
      Output:   impact scope, affected stages
      Activates: when existing work is modified (bugfix, change, hotfix)

  Engineering discipline stages — one responsibility each, reusable
  across every project:

    BDD:    Behavior-Driven Development
      Purpose:  Define acceptance criteria in Given/When/Then format.
                Bridge between requirements and implementation.
      Owner:    QA
      Input:    requirements, analysis
      Output:   BDD scenarios, acceptance criteria
      Activates: when requirements need formal acceptance criteria

    ARCH:   Architecture Verification
      Purpose:  Verify that implementation conforms to architecture
                decisions and principles.
      Owner:    AR
      Input:    implementation, architecture decision
      Output:   architecture review, compliance report
      Activates: after BE/API/FE/MD implementation, before QA

    DB:     Database
      Purpose:  Schema, data, and migration changes.
      Owner:    DB
      Input:    requirements, architecture
      Output:   schema, migrations, seed data
      Activates: when data model changes are required

    BE:     Backend
      Purpose:  Backend service and business-logic implementation.
      Owner:    BE
      Input:    requirements, BDD, DB schema
      Output:   backend code, API endpoints
      Activates: when backend implementation is required

    API:    API / Contract
      Purpose:  API contract, integration, and compatibility.
      Owner:    API
      Input:    requirements, backend implementation
      Output:   API contract, integration tests
      Activates: when API surface changes or integration is required

    FE:     Frontend
      Purpose:  Web frontend implementation.
      Owner:    FE
      Input:    requirements, API contract
      Output:   frontend code, UI components
      Activates: when UI changes are required

    MD:     Mobile
      Purpose:  Mobile application implementation.
      Owner:    MD
      Input:    requirements, API contract
      Output:   mobile code, platform-specific components
      Activates: when mobile changes are required

    QA:     Quality Assurance
      Purpose:  Testing and quality validation.
      Owner:    QA
      Input:    implementation, BDD scenarios
      Output:   test results, quality report
      Activates: after implementation, before SC

    SC:     Security
      Purpose:  Security validation (cross-cutting gate, not just
                another implementation stage).
      Owner:    SC
      Input:    implementation, threat model
      Output:   security findings, remediation tasks
      Activates: after QA, before DO — and continuously during
                 implementation for security-impacting work

    DO:     DevOps
      Purpose:  Build, deployment, infrastructure validation.
      Owner:    DO
      Input:    implementation, QA results
      Output:   deployment, infrastructure config
      Activates: after QA/SC pass, before VR

    VR:     Verification
      Purpose:  Final system verification and acceptance.
      Owner:    VR
      Input:    all stage outputs, test results
      Output:   verification report, acceptance decision
      Activates: after all required stages complete — VR is the final
                 stage of any chain (bax chains/chains.sdd -> Important)

    CR:     Code Review
      Purpose:  Peer review of implementation before merge.
      Owner:    AR
      Input:    implementation, decision, task context
      Output:   review report, approval, change requests
      Activates: after BE/API/FE/MD implementation and tests pass

    BRU:    Business Requirement Unit
      Purpose:  Validate implementation against business requirements.
      Owner:    HUMAN
      Input:    implementation, business requirements
      Output:   business acceptance decision
      Activates: after CR passes, before manual check

    CRU:    Code Requirement Unit
      Purpose:  Validate implementation against code requirements and standards.
      Owner:    QA
      Input:    implementation, code standards, architecture decisions
      Output:   code quality report, compliance decision
      Activates: after BRU passes, before manual check

    BE_TS:  Backend Test Suite
      Purpose:  Backend unit, integration, and contract tests.
      Owner:    QA
      Input:    backend implementation
      Output:   test report, coverage metrics
      Activates: after BE implementation, before CR

    FE_TS:  Frontend Test Suite
      Purpose:  Frontend component, integration, and E2E tests.
      Owner:    QA
      Input:    frontend implementation
      Output:   test report, coverage metrics
      Activates: after FE implementation, before CR

    MD_TS:  Mobile Test Suite
      Purpose:  Mobile platform, integration, and offline tests.
      Owner:    QA
      Input:    mobile implementation
      Output:   test report, coverage metrics
      Activates: after MD implementation, before CR


Phase124StateMapping:
  Purpose:
    Maps the PHASE 124 10-state task lifecycle to SDLC/STLC stages.
    The task lifecycle is the runtime engine state; SDLC stages are the
    engineering disciplines executed within those states.

  StateToStageMap:
    DISCOVERING    -> AN          (Analyze: scope, requirements, constraints)
    PLANNING       -> AR, BDD     (Architect: design, boundaries; BDD: acceptance criteria)
    IMPLEMENTING   -> DB, BE, API, FE, MD  (Engineering: database, backend, API, frontend, mobile)
    TESTING        -> BE_TS, FE_TS, MD_TS, QA  (Testing: per-layer test suites + quality assurance)
    FIXING         -> (returns to TESTING)  (Failure classification -> targeted fix -> retest)
    REVIEWING      -> ARCH, CR, BRU, CRU  (Architecture review, code review, business/quality gates)
    DOCUMENTING    -> D1          (Docs arm: human-readable documentation)
    LEARNING       -> Knowledge Gate  (Evaluate learning gate: NEW_KNOWLEDGE | EXISTING_UPDATED | ...)
    COMPLETED      -> VR          (Final verification and acceptance)
    BLOCKED        -> (suspended)  (External dependency or decision pending)
    ESCALATED      -> (human)     (Autonomy ceiling reached; human intervention required)

  SubStateTransitions:
    TESTING -> FAILED -> FIXING -> TESTING  (bounded by failure_budget and loop_score)
    IMPLEMENTING -> TESTING -> REVIEWING -> COMPLETED
    All states can -> BLOCKED (on dependency/decision stall)
    All states can -> ESCALATED (on autonomy ceiling breach)

  TaskLifecycleTrace:
    #01  DISCOVERING    -> Load task context (context-builder.sdd, memory-model.sdd)
    #02  PLANNING       -> Create plan, validate DAG (tasks.sdd, chains/graph.sdd)
    #03  IMPLEMENTING   -> Write code (project/ scaffold, stage outputs)
    #04  TESTING        -> Run tests (TEST-DISCOVERY-GATE, CHANGE-FILES-ONLY rule)
    #05  REVIEWING      -> Human gates (BRU, CRU, MANUAL-QA-GATE)
    #06  FIXING         -> Failure -> Classify -> Fix -> Re-test (LOOP-PROTECTION)
    #07  DOCUMENTING    -> Generate docs/ (HUMAN-DOCS-GATE)
    #08  LEARNING       -> Knowledge gate (LEARNING-GATE, memory-consolidation.sdd)
    #09  COMPLETED      -> Done proof (DONE-PROOF.sdd, VR)
    #10  BLOCKED/ESCALATED -> Human notification (AUTONOMY-POLICY.sdd)

  Navigation:
    TaskStateMachine: @../tasks/TASK-STATE-MACHINE.sdd
    LoopProtection: @../tasks/LOOP-PROTECTION.sdd
    FailureClassification: @../tasks/FAILURE-CLASSIFICATION.sdd
    AutonomyPolicy: @../tasks/AUTONOMY-POLICY.sdd
    DoneProof: @../tasks/DONE-PROOF.sdd
    LearningGate: @../workflow/learning-gate.sdd
    HumanDocsGate: @../workflow/human-docs-gate.sdd
    ExecutionContract: @../tasks/EXECUTION-CONTRACT.sdd
    ExecutionLoop: @../workflow/execution-loop.sdd

State: +
   (extended with PHASE 124 10-state task lifecycle mapping to SDLC/STLC stages)
   (added sub-state transitions, task lifecycle trace, and cross-references)
  
